|From 1475ecb7ad37eb77d219a2a720292db43748d150 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
|From: Felipe De Bene <benfelip@amazon.com>
|Date: Fri, 17 Oct 2026 12:30:00 +0000
|Subject: Haiku: keep the sign of a negative errno in ERR_GET_REASON
|
|Haiku's errno values are negative by default: EAGAIN is B_WOULD_BLOCK,
|INT_MIN + 11, i.e. 0x8000000b. err_set_error() stores a system error as
|ERR_SYSTEM_FLAG | reason and ERR_SYSTEM_FLAG is 0x80000000, so for a Haiku
|errno the flag bit and the errno's own sign bit are the same bit and the
|stored word is the errno unchanged -- nothing is lost on the way in.
|
|ERR_GET_REASON() then returns errcode & ERR_SYSTEM_MASK, and ERR_SYSTEM_MASK
|is INT_MAX, which strips exactly that bit. EAGAIN comes back as 11, so every
|caller that compares the result against an errno fails to match:
|BIO_err_is_non_fatal() classes a would-block as fatal, which makes the QUIC
|stack tear down a connection on the first empty non-blocking recvmmsg, and
|ossl_err_string_int() prints "No Error (11)" for it.
|
|So recover the sign rather than discarding it. Under B_USE_POSITIVE_POSIX_ERRORS
|the errno values are negated and therefore positive, the flag bit is genuinely
|just a flag, and upstream's masking is already correct -- hence the guard.
|
|0x80000000 is deliberately read as an errno and not as "reason 0". The two are
|the same stored word and cannot be told apart, but only one of them is real: it
|is ENOMEM (B_NO_MEMORY, which headers/os/support/Errors.h singles out as the
|code that "can't be negated"), whereas a system error raised with errno 0 says
|nothing anyone can act on. Reading it as 0 would turn an out-of-memory into
|"No Error", which is the failure class this patch exists to stop.
|
|crypto/err/err.c needs no matching change: its only ERR_GET_REASON consumer for
|a system error passes the value to openssl_strerror_r(int, char *, size_t),
|which truncates it back to the correct 32 bits.
|
|--- a/include/openssl/err.h.in
|+++ b/include/openssl/err.h.in
--------------------------
